A. Describe the pathways of lipoprotein metabolism:
A(i) the exogenous pathway;
A(ii) the endogenous pathway;
A(iii) the LDL receptor and scavenger pathways;
A(iv) reverse cholesterol transport.
B. B(i) Explain how cells regulate their free cholesterol and why small dense LDL is especially atherogenic.
B(ii) State the anti-atherosclerotic properties of HDL and factors that change HDL.

A. Describe tumour markers under the following headings:
A(i) definition, biochemistry and specificity;
A(ii) factors influencing plasma levels;
A(iii) the ideal marker;
A(iv) clinical uses.
B. B(i) Classify tumour markers with examples.
B(ii) Distinguish AFP, CEA and hCG.
B(iii) State the methods of assay.

A. Describe immunodeficiency under the following headings:
A(i) definition and classification;
A(ii) severe combined immunodeficiency;
A(iii) DiGeorge syndrome;
A(iv) Bruton's disease.
B. B(i) Describe the causes of secondary immunodeficiency.
B(ii) Describe how infections cause immunodeficiency.
B(iii) Explain how thymic hypoplasia affects other lymphoid tissues and antibody levels.

A. Describe lipoproteins under the following headings:
A(i) definition and structure;
A(ii) classification;
A(iii) apolipoproteins and their roles;
A(iv) lipoprotein(a).
B. B(i) Explain the clinical basis for the association of lipids and lipoproteins with cardiovascular disease.
B(ii) Distinguish between LDL and HDL and between small dense and large buoyant LDL.

A. Describe oxidants and reactive species under the following headings:
A(i) definition and types of oxidant;
A(ii) reactive oxygen and nitrogen species;
A(iii) endogenous and exogenous sources;
A(iv) the reactions that generate free radicals.
B. B(i) State the beneficial roles of oxidants and free radicals.
B(ii) Describe the antioxidant defence system.

A. Describe the evaluation of B-cell/antibody function under the following headings:
A(i) the steps and clinical indicators of a humoral defect;
A(ii) the first laboratory step;
A(iii) in vivo assays;
A(iv) B-cell numbers and lymphoid tissue histology.
B. B(i) Describe the in vitro assays.
B(ii) Distinguish absence of B cells from functional defects of B cells.

A. Describe the adrenal gland under the following headings:
A(i) location and structure;
A(ii) the zones of the cortex, their hormones and actions;
A(iii) the medulla and catecholamine synthesis and metabolism.
B. B(i) State how the zones and medulla relate to the common disorders of hypersecretion and hyposecretion.
B(ii) Explain how disease of the hypothalamus, pituitary or long-term steroid therapy causes adrenal insufficiency and its functional deficits.

A. Describe the thyroid gland under the following headings:
A(i) anatomy and functional unit;
A(ii) hormones secreted;
A(iii) the steps of hormone biosynthesis.
B. B(i) Describe the transport of thyroid hormones and explain why changes in binding proteins alter total but not free hormone levels.
B(ii) Distinguish T3 from T4 and describe the peripheral metabolism of thyroid hormones.

A. Describe the assessment of cell-mediated immunity under the following headings:
A(i) clinical significance of T-cell defects;
A(ii) enumeration and skin testing;
A(iii) functional assays and NK cell assessment.
B. B(i) Describe the assessment of phagocyte function.
B(ii) Describe the assessment of the complement system.

A. Describe male reproductive biology under the following headings:
A(i) testicular structure;
A(ii) Leydig and Sertoli cells and their feedback control;
A(iii) the sources, actions and cellular mechanisms of testosterone.
B. B(i) Describe male sexual development and testosterone levels through life.
B(ii) Explain how testicular or pituitary-hypothalamic disease produces hypogonadism and altered gonadotrophins.

A. Describe the pituitary gland under the following headings:
A(i) location, size and connections;
A(ii) the cell populations of the anterior lobe and their hormones;
A(iii) the posterior lobe.
B. B(i) Classify pituitary tumours and state their relative frequencies.
B(ii) Explain how pituitary disease causes visual and hormonal deficits.

A. Describe the menstrual cycle and its hormonal control under the following headings:
A(i) definition, length and follicle numbers;
A(ii) the follicular phase;
A(iii) ovulation;
A(iv) the luteal phase and what follows.
B. B(i) Explain the two-cell, two-gonadotrophin theory.
B(ii) State the biochemical basis for the day-21 progesterone test, the oestrogens and the hormonal changes of menopause.

A. Describe autoimmunity under the following headings:
A(i) definition and features;
A(ii) how self-tolerance fails;
A(iii) the theories of induction;
A(iv) genetic contribution.
B. B(i) Explain the clinical basis of pathogenic autoantibodies and familial transfer.
B(ii) Distinguish organ-specific from systemic autoimmune disease.
B(iii) State the treatment approaches.

A. Describe immunological tolerance under the following headings:
A(i) definition and importance;
A(ii) the mechanisms of tolerance;
A(iii) central tolerance;
A(iv) peripheral tolerance.
B. B(i) Distinguish central from peripheral tolerance.
B(ii) Explain why immunologically privileged sites and sequestered antigens matter clinically.
